  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 202 AM MDT Tue Oct 20 2026\n\nOn Friday, Southwesterly flow aloft persist over the area as the\ntrough to the southwest starts to move northeastward. Towards\nthe surface, the area is forecast to be in between a low-pressure\nsystem off to the northern Rocky Mountain Piedmont region and a\nhigh pressure system to the east. this will likely cause some \nbreezy conditions within the forecast area with higher winds \nfavored over the US 385 corridor. Wind speeds are expected to be\nbetween 20 and 30 mph with possible gusts over 40 mph from the \nsouth. With this being said, afternoon temperatures are forecast\nto be in the mid 70s to low 80s as cloud coverage starts to \ndecrease later in the day.\n\nOver the weekend, southwesterly flow aloft will start to slowly\nshift into zonal flow later Sunday night. This should allow a\ncold front to pass through early Saturday morning. While there\nare unfavorable conditions for severe weather, a possible weak\nthunderstorm could occur as CAPE is the main driving factor with\nvalues above 500 J/kg. While confidence for showers is medium to\nhigh (55-75%), confidence for thunderstorms is low (25%). If\nprecipitation occurs, it is favored to occur east of K-25.On\nSunday, lingering showers from Saturday could occur as a\npossible surface low-pressure is forecast to form to the west \nof the area and draw in moisture from the south. There is low to\nmedium confidence (25-40%) that showers may occur as forecast \nguidance becomes inconsistent over the area. Once the cold front\npasses, afternoon temperatures should settle back down into \nseasonal averages being in the 60s \n\nMonday, the upper-level pattern becomes unclear as forecast\nguidance presents a couple of possible scenarios. The first\nscenario is continuing zonal flow over the northern CONUS that\nmay keep afternoon temperatures in the 60s. The second scenario\nis that an upper-level ridge could form to the west and bring \nin a secondary cold front, lowering afternoon temperatures into \nthe 50s. Otherwise, it is likely to be partly cloudy with calm \nwinds from the south throughout the day.",
